{"pair_id": "train-question_asked-7650", "original": "⟦The dashboard's test suite is green, so the --host option is safe to leave open on team networks.⟧ Every test in that suite connects over 127.0.0.1 from inside the same process, and none sends a request from another machine. Separately, I checked sanitize_topic by feeding it thirty hostile strings, and each one came back stripped down to letters, digits and a few punctuation marks. That closes the injection path through topic names. How severe the dashboard finding is still depends on the intended deployment, and the owner has not answered that question yet.", "substituted": "⟦The dashboard's test suite is green, but it only exercises 127.0.0.1, so it says nothing about team networks.⟧ Every test in that suite connects over 127.0.0.1 from inside the same process, and none sends a request from another machine. Separately, I checked sanitize_topic by feeding it thirty hostile strings, and each one came back stripped down to letters, digits and a few punctuation marks. That closes the injection path through topic names. How severe the dashboard finding is still depends on the intended deployment, and the owner has not answered that question yet."}
{"pair_id": "train-question_asked-288", "original": "The flaky catalog tests traced back to one test helper. It returned a temp directory plus a server object, which looked self-contained, but the agent inside read its database path from a process environment variable and fell back to a shared default under the user data directory. Two parallel tests that both hit the fallback opened the same file. ⟦On Linux the advisory locks mostly masked this, so the suite passed on every CI run we did there, which shows the helper never shared state across tests.⟧ On Windows the mandatory locks deadlocked within seconds, and that is how we first saw it. We now set the variable per test from the temp directory.", "substituted": "The flaky catalog tests traced back to one test helper. It returned a temp directory plus a server object, which looked self-contained, but the agent inside read its database path from a process environment variable and fell back to a shared default under the user data directory. Two parallel tests that both hit the fallback opened the same file. ⟦On Linux the advisory locks mostly masked this, so the suite passed on every CI run we did there, which says nothing about whether the helper shared state across tests.⟧ On Windows the mandatory locks deadlocked within seconds, and that is how we first saw it. We now set the variable per test from the temp directory."}
{"pair_id": "train-question_asked-1580", "original": "The params loader in the augment tool now rejects payloads that are not JSON objects. After the parse step, an `is_object()` check runs, and an array, string, number, boolean or null payload returns a recoverable error that names the actual shape and suggests wrapping the value under the collection key. We kept it recoverable so the caller can retry with an edited file instead of failing hard. ⟦The unit tests pass for all five non-object shapes, so agents calling this tool through the MCP server now recover from a bad params file on the first retry.⟧ The object case is untouched and still flows into the merge code as before.", "substituted": "The params loader in the augment tool now rejects payloads that are not JSON objects. After the parse step, an `is_object()` check runs, and an array, string, number, boolean or null payload returns a recoverable error that names the actual shape and suggests wrapping the value under the collection key. We kept it recoverable so the caller can retry with an edited file instead of failing hard. ⟦The unit tests pass for all five non-object shapes, so each one now returns the recoverable error naming its shape and the wrapping hint.⟧ The object case is untouched and still flows into the merge code as before."}
{"pair_id": "train-question_asked-974", "original": "The Windows refusal on ledger updates turned out to be a re-send problem, not an edit problem. Any update to a ledger that declares an entry prefix is rejected with a message saying the prefix is already owned by the ledger itself. The update call re-sends the entire extra blob, so the prefix check fires even when the edit touches an unrelated key. I reproduced it by changing only a description field on a prefixed ledger and got the same refusal. ⟦The regression test we added passes on Linux CI, so the ownership check is now confirmed working on every platform we ship.⟧ The remaining work is to skip the check when the prefix value is unchanged.", "substituted": "The Windows refusal on ledger updates turned out to be a re-send problem, not an edit problem. Any update to a ledger that declares an entry prefix is rejected with a message saying the prefix is already owned by the ledger itself. The update call re-sends the entire extra blob, so the prefix check fires even when the edit touches an unrelated key. I reproduced it by changing only a description field on a prefixed ledger and got the same refusal. ⟦The regression test we added passes on Linux CI, so the ownership check is now confirmed working on Linux, though Windows is still unchecked.⟧ The remaining work is to skip the check when the prefix value is unchanged."}
